  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/2932639855f3031da6adcca7eaee38b992a145809f119902e93958fee47ff2a6.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $\u0394$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\u0394={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}\u2061v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}\u2061v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the left side of $\u0394$ under the action of $T?$",
  "worked_solution": "To map the entire region $\u0394$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the left side.\n\nFor the left side, we have $u=1$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}\u2061v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}\u2061v) \\\\  & =(1\\operatorname{cos}\u2061v,\\,1\\operatorname{sin}\u2061v) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}\u2061v),\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an equation of a circular arc of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. \nThe arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/b829ff7e7a51ed9f8089b0b8fc22f727ccf5dc7b2e937ec90f6a1affb8d29064.png)",
